    {
      "id": "174027c6-00f3-4de8-b196-fcd2009cce5c",
      "name": "MCP is awesome when people stay in control",
      "path": "WWW / The MCP Journal / MCP is awesome when people stay in control",
      "docTypeId": "b4b3f778-e280-4e2e-bfdb-b327999d07e1",
      "templateId": "f4447b67-9d3c-4400-8ca5-a48d44eae82c",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "fbb166b1-47b8-456d-a531-b3e35786ad43",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "d122e625-7552-473c-b781-d91f1bf732b4",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "3f9e569a-2f13-4740-b92a-9b82241aa66f",
                "key": "fbb166b1-47b8-456d-a531-b3e35786ad43",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "eyebrow",
                    "value": "BUILDING WITH CONFIDENCE"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "heading",
                    "value": "MCP is awesome when people stay in control"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "summary",
                    "value": "The best MCP workflows combine automation, transparent changes, and room for human judgment."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "3b8ae412-78cd-4031-9718-534977d8b67b",
                        "mediaKey": "c74f2da3-0d56-4017-9b34-4032abbb61ba",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "imageCredit",
                    "value": "Photo by Vardan Papikyan on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "a05ecd24-d91b-433e-ba86-9b406004eec5",
                "key": "d122e625-7552-473c-b781-d91f1bf732b4",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>It is easy to imagine an assistant doing more with tools. The more interesting question is how that work remains understandable. MCP helps by making capabilities explicit: a server offers named operations, structured inputs, and responses that can be inspected.</p><h2>Small steps are easier to trust</h2><p>Consider publishing a blog. We can first examine the existing content tree, then create reusable block types, configure a block grid, add images to a media folder, and finally publish pages. Reading the result after each structural change catches mismatched keys and missing values early.</p><p>That stepwise approach is useful well beyond content management. A team can connect local tools for design, documentation, issue tracking, or testing, while still deciding which operations belong in an automated flow and which decisions need a person.</p><h2>Reusable structure opens creative space</h2><p>Blocks are a good match for this way of working. Editors can rearrange a hero, an article, or a list of posts without rebuilding the site. Developers keep one rendering path and one set of components. The assistant can create content through the same schema the backoffice understands.</p><p>MCP is awesome because it makes that collaboration tangible. The protocol does not supply taste or judgment by itself; it makes the right tools available at the right moment, with enough structure to check the outcome.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "fbb166b1-47b8-456d-a531-b3e35786ad43",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
